# Small, because the point of the declaration is that the runner stops the
# suite rather than that the number is large — and not one second, because the
# clock starts when the suite's *process* does (`commands::test::execute`).
# Building the suite is not on the budget; starting it is, and starting a
# process is the machine's cost rather than the suite's: on a loaded machine
# the window between spawning a freshly linked binary and its first line of
# `main` has been measured at several hundred milliseconds. The twin below has
# to *finish* inside this same number, so a budget that only just covers what
# the machine charges is a budget a busy machine can miss, and the case then
# fails for a reason it is not about. Both halves keep one declaration: one
# variable changes between them, and it is the loop.
library {
    sources: ["spin.buri"]

    test {
        sources: ["test/spin.buri"]
        timeout_seconds: 5
    }
}
